ork'\n70\t  - ✓ HTML list of 5 bus services with prices and details\n71\t  - ✓ Monthly cost breakdown with 7 expense categories\n72\t  - ✓ Internal links to /getting-here and /rooms\n73\t  - ✓ readTime '8 min read', date '2026-04-14'\n74\t  - ✓ npm run build passes\n75\t\n76\t## Iteration US-243 - Mon, Apr 14, 2026\n77\t\n78\tCompleted: Blog post - Singapore Work Pass Guide for Malaysians (ID: US-243)\n79\t\n80\t### What was implemented\n81\t- **Blog post** (`site/src/data/blog/posts.ts`):\n82\t  - Added new blog post object with slug `singapore-work-pass-guide-malaysians`\n83\t  - Category: \"Career & Work\", date: \"2026-04-14\", readTime: \"12 min read\"\n84\t  - Comprehensive HTML comparison table of 3 Singapore work pass types:\n85\t    - Employment Pass (EP): Minimum salary SGD 5,000/month\n86\t    - S Pass: Minimum salary SGD 3,150/month\n87\t    - Work Permit: No fixed minimum, sector-specific\n88\t  - 5 Q&A items in FAQ section covering common questions:\n89\t    - Diploma vs EP eligibility\n90\t    - Salary negotiation strategy\n91\t    - Family dependencies policies\n92\t    - Actual processing timelines\n93\t    - Job changing mid-pass restrictions\n94\t  - Additional sections on red flags, individual pass details, and next steps\n95\t\n96\t### Patterns discovered\n97\t- Blog posts are stored as static TypeScript objects in `rawPosts` array\n98\t- Reading time is auto-computed from word count (200 wpm baseline)\n99\t- HTML content is embedded as template literals in TypeScript strings\n100\t- Blog metadata includes slug, title, excerpt, date, category, author, readTime, tags, featuredImage\n101\t\n102\t### Gotchas\n103\t- Content HTML must stay under 8000 characters for performance\n104\t- All blog post fields are required; missing fields cause TypeScript compilation errors\n105\t- Category must match existing categories or create new ones (checked against unique set)\n106\t\n107\t### Test results\n108\t- Content size: 6,066 characters (well under 8,000 limit)\n109\t- TypeScript compilation: ✓ passes (npx tsc --noEmit)\n110\t- Next.js build: ✓ succeeds (67 static pages including new blog post)\n111\t- Blog post accessible via /blog/singapore-work-pass-guide-malaysians slug\n112\t- All acceptance criteria met:\n113\t  - ✓ Slug and category correct\n114\t  - ✓ Comparison table with all 3 pass types and salary minimums\n115\t  - ✓ FAQ section with 5 Q&As (exceeds 3 required)\n116\t  - ✓ ReadTime '12 min read', date '2026-04-14'\n117\t  - ✓ npm run build passes\n118\t\n119\t## Iteration US-196 - Fri, Apr 14, 2026\n120\t\n121\tCompleted: Integrate channel manager for live OTA availability synchronisation (ID: US-196)\n122\t\n123\t### What was implemented\n124\t- **Beds24 client** (`site/src/lib/beds24-client.ts`):\n125\t  - Implemented `parseAvailabilityResponse()` to parse real Beds24 API responses\n126\t  - Returns proper `ChannelManagerAvailability` objects with room inventory\n127\t  - Graceful fallback to placeholder data when channel manager is not configured\n128\t- **ARI endpoint** (`site/src/app/api/ari/route.ts`):\n129\t  - Fetches live availability from Beds24 API\n130\t  - Maps to standard ARI format for connectivity partners (Google Free Booking Links)\n131\t  - Returns JSON with property info, rooms, rates, and booking URL\n132\t- **Booking sync endpoint** (`site/src/app/api/bookings/sync/route.ts`):\n133\t  - Accepts POST requests with booking details (checkin, checkout, guest info, room type)\n134\t  - Syncs bookings to Beds24 to close availability across OTAs\n135\t  - Includes availability check via GET with query parameters\n136\t- **Webhook endpoint** (`site/src/app/api/webhooks/beds24/route.ts`):\n137\t  - Processes Beds24 webhook events for availability updates, booking creation/cancellation\n138\t  - Validates webhook signature using HMAC-SHA256\n139\t  - Logs event details (extensible for database updates)\n140\t- **Configuration & types**:\n141\t  - Channel manager config with environment variables\n142\t  - Type definitions for availability, bookings, webhooks\n143\t  - Updated .env.local.example with all required secrets\n144\t\n145\t### Patterns discovered\n146\t- Next.js App Router uses async GET/POST functions\n147\t- Beds24 API response structure uses `roomtypes` key with room availability objects\n148\t- Response parsing should handle multiple field names (id/roomId, name/roomName) for flexibility\n149\t- Placeholder data allows testing without configured channel manager\n150\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"64023916-cf9d-44b1-9e84-df8466e38e83","uuid":"c40eaf1e-3458-433b-958b-8bc5ed6ad8ea","timestamp":"2026-04-13T21:56:34.644Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\Software Projects\\pelangi-website\\progress.txt","content":"## Iteration US-249 - Mon, Apr 14, 2026\n\nCompleted: Blog post - 5 Gig Jobs for Malaysians Waiting for Singapore Work Approval (ID: US-249)\n\n### What was implemented\n- **Blog post** (`site/src/data/blog/posts.ts`):\n  - Added new blog post object with slug `gig-jobs-malaysia-waiting-singapore-work-pass`\n  - Category: \"Career & Work\", date: \"2026-04-14\", readTime: \"9 min read\"\n  - 5 gig economy job options with earnings estimates:\n    - Foodpanda Delivery Rider: RM1,800–3,500/month\n    - Lalamove Driver: RM2,000–3,800/month\n    - Grab Ride/GrabFood Driver: RM2,500–4,200/month\n    - Part-time F&B/Cafe Work: RM1,500–2,500/month\n    - Online Freelancing: RM1,200–5,000+/month\n  - Each job includes: requirements, earnings breakdown, pros, and cons\n  - Sections on combining gigs, tax/legal notes, Pelangi guest 